        "problem": "Find ${y}^{\u2032}$ for $y=3({x}^{2}-2tan\u2061x)+4.$",
        "worked_solution": "We know that $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(tan\u2061x)={sec}^{2}\u2061x.$ Applying this formula, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{\u2032} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(3({x}^{2}-2tan\u2061x)+4) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(3{x}^{2}-6tan\u2061x+4) \\\\  & =3\\cdot 2x-6{sec}^{2}\u2061x+0 \\\\  & =6x-6{sec}^{2}\u2061x.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
